<div class="glass-card" style="max-width: 800px; margin: 3rem auto; padding: 2.5rem;">
    <h1 style="color: var(--primary); margin-bottom: 1.5rem; text-align: center; border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.1); padding-bottom: 1.5rem; line-height: 1.3;">
        The Story Of Noble Polyhedron: Exploring The Hidden Realm Of Absolute Symmetry
    </h1>

    <div style="margin-bottom: 2rem;">
        <p style="color: rgba(255,255,255,0.85); line-height: 1.8; margin-bottom: 1.5rem; font-size: 1.05rem;">
            When we first learn 3D geometry, we are familiarized with the basic polyhedrons such as cubes, pyramids, spheres, and the five Platonic solids that are considered to be highly symmetric. However, there is a lesser-known class of shapes called Noble Polyhedra that are rarely covered online or described beyond abstract mathematical calculations. I created this website called Noble Polyhedron to visualize these fascinating shapes.
        </p>

        <p style="color: rgba(255,255,255,0.85); line-height: 1.8; margin-bottom: 1.5rem; font-size: 1.05rem;">
            To put it briefly noble polyhedrons are isohedral (face-transitive) and isogonal (vertex-transitive) polyhedra, and they are not necessarily isohedral with respect to their edges. This means that all of their vertices are the same, and all of their faces are the same, but their edges can be different. All regular polyhedrons are considered noble polyhedrons. However, the category of noble polyhedrons also includes self-intersecting shapes like Kepler-Poinsot star polyhedrons, crown polyhedrons (also called stephanoids), and toroidal (doughnut-shaped) polyhedrons in which vertices and faces are equal in count and symmetric in shape. Since all noble polyhedrons are isohedral and isogonal, all of them are either self-dual or have another noble polyhedron that shares the same number of faces, edges, and vertices.
        </p>

        <p style="color: rgba(255,255,255,0.85); line-height: 1.8; margin-bottom: 1.5rem; font-size: 1.05rem;">
            Noble Polyhedron began as an idea to develop a website dedicated to the study of these fascinating geometrical shapes. The problem that I encountered with the already existing pages dedicated to polyhedrons is that they either cover only the basics such as Platonic solids or are too niche and require advanced mathematical knowledge to be understood. Noble Polyhedron is a collaborative web project that was created as part of Webtigo applications for the development of digital products and tools for the web. Although the site is part of the Webtigo, I wanted to develop something unique and independent, and not just add another subpage to the main website. When I first told my brother Akshat about the idea of developing a web page dedicated to noble polyhedrons, he suggested that it would be a great idea to expand the page into a unique tool that could help you explore these beautiful shapes. We spent a lot of time working on this idea and finally managed to implement an exciting and exciting project for all web developers and geometry lovers.
        </p>

        <p style="color: rgba(255,255,255,0.85); line-height: 1.8; margin-bottom: 1.5rem; font-size: 1.05rem;">
            Looking at the system of three-dimensional geometry as a whole, you can see that among the various polyhedra there are families of noble polyhedra. These are the five regular Platonic solids: tetrahedron, cube, octahedron, dodecahedron, and icosahedron. The simplest Platonic solid is a tetrahedron with only 4 triangular faces. All other regular polyhedrons are dual polyhedrons, which means that vertices and faces change places. For example, octahedron vertices correspond to cube faces, and cube vertices correspond to octahedron faces. Similarly, dodecahedron vertices correspond to icosahedron faces and vice versa.
        </p>

        <p style="color: rgba(255,255,255,0.85); line-height: 1.8; margin-bottom: 1.5rem; font-size: 1.05rem;">
            Beyond the regular convex polyhedrons, there are also four non-convex noble polyhedrons called Kepler-Poinsot star polyhedrons. Unlike regular polyhedrons, the faces of Kepler-Poinsot polyhedrons intersect at angles. This way, their faces create the shape of a star. They are called small stellated dodecahedron, great stellated dodecahedron, great dodecahedron, and great icosahedron. All of their faces are regular polygons, and all of their vertices are identical.
        </p>

        <p style="color: rgba(255,255,255,0.85); line-height: 1.8; margin-bottom: 1.5rem; font-size: 1.05rem;">
            Beyond regular and star polyhedrons, there are also non-regular noble polyhedrons. The most well-known of these polyhedrons are called crown polyhedrons or stephanoids. They are unique in that their skew faces form a ring, similar to a crown, with intersecting faces. Although the edges of stephanoids differ in length and direction, all of their vertices and faces are equal. Grünbaum, one of the mathematicians who studied noble polyhedrons, also discovered that there are noble toroids.
        </p>

        <p style="color: rgba(255,255,255,0.85); line-height: 1.8; margin-bottom: 1.5rem; font-size: 1.05rem;">
            Noble Toroids also have equal faces, but they form a closed ring of a doughnut shape. The existence of noble toroids is interesting because it shows that noble polyhedrons can also have topological genus greater than zero.
        </p>

        <p style="color: rgba(255,255,255,0.85); line-height: 1.8; margin-bottom: 1.5rem; font-size: 1.05rem;">
            Beyond all of these categories, mathematicians have also long wondered if infinite-hedrons (infinite polyhedrons) can also be regular and noble. An infinite polyhedron is a structure that extends to infinity. However, it still follows the same rules as regular polyhedrons that we know and have studied.
        </p>

        <p style="color: rgba(255,255,255,0.85); line-height: 1.8; margin-bottom: 1.5rem; font-size: 1.05rem;">
            There are two types of infinite regular polyhedrons. The first type is triply periodic skew apeirohedrons. They are called infinite polyhedrons, but they also have a structure similar to polyhedrons and are sometimes called sponge-like infinite polyhedrons. They were first classified by mathematicians H.S.M. Coxeter and John Flinders Petrie. They are called triply periodic because they are periodic in three directions — horizontally, vertically, and perpendicular to the surface. For example, squares can form an infinite polyhedron that continues indefinitely in all directions. Each square shares edges with other squares, and all vertices have 6 squares around them, just like in regular cubes. This infinite polyhedron also has a dual, which is formed from hexagons. Because each hexagon shares vertices and edges with other identical hexagons, they are infinite-hedrons and follow the rules of noble polyhedrons.
        </p>

        <p style="color: rgba(255,255,255,0.85); line-height: 1.8; margin-bottom: 1.5rem; font-size: 1.05rem;">
            The second type of infinite-hedrons are the infinite crown ladders. If you take a finite crown (a stephanoid that has a ring of faces) and make the number of steps or vertices in the ring approach infinity, you will get an infinite crown ladder. The infinite crown ladders are similar structures to the infinite-hedrons described above, but their congruent faces continue climbing up and around either an infinitely long cylinder or a helix. Since every step has congruent faces and shares edges and vertices with the same faces, they are all examples of noble polyhedrons.
        </p>

        <p style="color: rgba(255,255,255,0.85); line-height: 1.8; margin-bottom: 1.5rem; font-size: 1.05rem;">
            My project Noble Polyhedron is dedicated to the study and popularization of noble polyhedrons. This project is an open invitation to delve into the fascinating world of geometry and mathematics. We hope that our website will familiarize you with the beautiful examples of noble polyhedrons and intrigue you with their beauty and complexity. Would you like to add a paragraph describing the 3D controls and features with which you can familiarize yourself with the models on the website?
        </p>
